# Build context for the four images (core, api, web, logger).
#
# The context is the repository root: without this file the builder receives
# .git, whatever node_modules a developer happens to have (133 MB in the
# reference checkout), build outputs and the whole docs/site/mobile trees — for
# every one of the four image builds. Two consequences, both real: the context
# transfer is slow, and `COPY web/frontend/ ./` in Dockerfile.web bakes a local
# node_modules and a stale dist into the image.
#
# Everything below is either regenerated inside the image or never COPYed by a
# Dockerfile. Keep `proto/`, `core/`, `api/`, `web/`, `rust/`, `requirements.txt`
# and `VERSION` reachable: the builds need them.

.git
.gitignore
.github
.hermes
.venv
.env
.env.*

**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/*.log

# Dependency trees and build outputs are produced in-image (npm ci, cargo build,
# flutter build); copying a host copy in only adds time and staleness. The web
# frontend's dist is built in the frontend stage and handed to the Rust stage with
# COPY --from, so it must not come from the context.
**/node_modules
**/dist
**/target
**/.dart_tool
mobile/
website/
# docs/* (not docs/) so the file-negation below actually re-includes it
# (dockerignore cannot traverse into a fully excluded directory). The API's
# own contract ships inside the api image: GET /api/openapi.yaml copies it
# next to the binary, so the published doc cannot drift from the serving code.
docs/*
!docs/openapi.yaml
k8s/
logger/
scripts/
*.md
